      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Studies typically identify friction points as stemming from perceived shifts in local business ownership, changes in neighborhood aesthetics, and noise or recreational lifestyle differences rather than intrinsic cultural incompatibility. While there are moments where trust erodes between residents, these breakdowns are generally short-term reactions to perceived threats against Establishment norms rather than permanent failures of community cohesion. Most analyses conclude that without proactive communication and shared governance, these initial adjustments can eventually evolve into more stable, albeit different, forms of neighborhood identity.",
